1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:08,000
Ten STL files. Thirteen printed pieces.
R2-D2 revision D | 317 mm body | three powered feet | two-foot and three-leg stances | Raspberry Pi 4 and KB2040 | 12 V 7 Ah battery

2
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:25,000
01 | Three feet, six motors, twelve wheels
Load two Adafruit 3777 motors and four 3766 wheels into each foot through the sole, then fit the motor tab bolts. Right and centre feet are shown cut away.

3
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:42,000
02 | Outer legs, two prints each
Slide the lower leg tongue into the foot slot, splice the upper leg over it, run two M8 rods the full length, then fit the ankle pivot and lock bolts. Each upper leg carries two brass lock receivers.

4
00:00:42,000 --> 00:00:55,000
03 | Centre-leg housing and caster
Press two bearings onto the centre-foot stem, drop the centre-leg housing over them, fit the M12 caster bolt and the swivel stop pin. The foot then swivels freely.

5
00:00:55,000 --> 00:01:10,000
04 | Lower ring, battery and power
Strap the 12 V 7 Ah battery to its shelf, then fit the speaker, the charge port and the main switch.

6
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:24,000
05 | Stance guide, carriage and actuator
Seat two 12 mm guide shafts in the lower ring. The printed carriage rides them on two LM12LUU bearings and pivots on the centre-leg housing. An Actuonix P16 actuator drives it along the guide.

7
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:37,000
06 | Upper ring, shoulders and sensed locks
Seat the upper ring and bolt both shoulders with M12 bolts. Each shoulder gets a sensed lock: a spring plunger, an MG995 release servo and an SS-01GL switch.

8
00:01:37,000 --> 00:01:49,000
07 | Integral electronics deck
Mount the Raspberry Pi 4, the KB2040, four DRV8833 drivers, the DRV8871 actuator driver, the level shifter, both regulators, the ADS1115 and the fuse blocks on the deck printed into the upper ring.

9
00:01:49,000 --> 00:01:59,000
08 | Join the two rings
The locating lip aligns the rings. Fit eight M4 seam bolts through the internal flange, then four M8 rods on radius 138 to clamp the whole stack.

10
00:01:59,000 --> 00:02:11,000
09 | Head friction drive
Bolt the drive base under the top plate. Fit the seventh motor and its wheel on the pivoting arm, then the spring, the M5 adjuster and the thumb nut.

11
00:02:11,000 --> 00:02:22,000
10 | Dome displays and lights
On the bench: the twelve-wire slip ring at the hub, the round TFT behind the radar eye, four logic matrices, two PSI jewels and three holoprojector LEDs.

12
00:02:22,000 --> 00:02:35,000
11 | Lazy susan and dome
Screw the lazy susan to the top plate, lower the dome onto it, then tighten the thumb nut until the drive wheel presses the dome plate by 0.6 mm.

13
00:02:35,000 --> 00:02:43,000
12 | Power on
Simulated: the logic displays, the holoprojectors and both PSI lights come up behind the dome cutouts, and the dome begins to rotate.

14
00:02:43,000 --> 00:02:58,000
13 | Simulated drive
Three-leg stance, both locks seated: forward 400 mm, a wide left arc with the centre foot castering to the path, stop, reverse 150 mm, stop, then nudge the dome left and right.

15
00:02:58,000 --> 00:03:10,000
14 | Stance change: three legs to two feet
Hold-to-run from the control page. Both locks release, the actuator tilts the body upright while the firmware rolls the centre foot back with it, the locks re-seat, then the centre foot lifts clear. Operator drive and the dome are refused throughout.

16
00:03:10,000 --> 00:03:16,000
15 | Two-foot stance
Stationary: body upright, centre foot 25 mm clear of the floor, both locks seated in their 0 degree receivers. Ground drive is refused.

17
00:03:16,000 --> 00:03:28,000
16 | Stance change: two feet to three legs
The locked centre foot lowers to the floor, both locks release, the actuator tilts the body back while the firmware rolls the centre foot forward, and the locks seat in the three-leg receivers. Drive is allowed again.

18
00:03:28,000 --> 00:03:34,000
Assembly complete | Revision D prototype design
742 mm tall | 317 mm body | ten STL files | thirteen printed pieces | physical fit, strength, driving and powered stance-change tests are still required. See the assembly manual.
